---
title: "Cantor crash course — a study guide"
description: "A study guide to Georg Cantor's mathematics of infinity: the Absolute Infinite, the transfinite, the Franzelin correspondence, and why 'infinite' is not one word."
date: 2026
tags: [study guide, philosophy]
---

This is the first non-theologian crash course on the site. A mathematician. Georg Cantor made the most precise distinction between God's infinity and creation's infinity ever put on paper &mdash; and he did it with proofs, not assertions. The relevance to everything else here is direct: "infinite" is not one word. The failure to distinguish two categorically different things that both get called "infinite" generates a pantheism worry that has haunted theology for centuries. If everything is infinite and God is infinite, they must be the same thing. Cantor dissolved that worry. He showed that what we mean by "infinite" when we talk about mathematical objects and what we mean by "infinite" when we talk about God are not two sizes of one thing. They are two different things entirely.

Georg Cantor was born in 1845 in St. Petersburg, raised in Germany, and spent his career as a professor at the University of Halle. He created set theory &mdash; the branch of mathematics that underlies virtually all of modern mathematics. He faced fierce opposition from Leopold Kronecker, who considered actual infinities illegitimate and worked to block Cantor's career. Cantor suffered severe mental breakdowns &mdash; real, documented, and not the focus of this guide. He died in 1918 in a sanatorium in Halle. The point here is the mathematics and the theological correspondence that grew out of it.

:::toc
1. [Two kinds of infinite](#two-kinds)
2. [The conceptual lineage](#lineage)
3. [The Franzelin correspondence](#franzelin)
4. [The honest wrinkle](#wrinkle)
